items.text:
A beginner’s walkthrough of running one SAP CAP application on SQLite locally and on SAP HANA Cloud in production using profiles. It includes the @SAP /xssec crash I hit on Cloud Foundry and how I fixed it. 
 Introduction 
 I’m new to the SAP Cloud Application Programming Model (CAP). One of my first tasks was to build an app, run it locally on SQLite, and deploy the same code to SAP HANA Cloud. It was a great way to learn how profiles, database drivers and Cloud Foundry fit together, and I hit a few errors along the way. 
 My use case is a small Library app with three master data entities: Books, Authors and Members. Sample data comes from CSV files in db/data/ , named <namespace>-<Entity>.csv . A wrong file name creates an empty table with no warning. 

 Development profile: SQLite 
 With a plain "db": "sqlite" , CAP runs SQLite in memory, so no database file exists. I wanted a file, so I added a path in package.json : json "cds": {
 "requires": {
 "db": {
 "[development]": {
 "kind": "sqlite",
 "credentials": { "url": "db/practice.db" }
 },
 "[production]": { "kind": "hana" }
 },
 "auth": { "kind": "dummy" }
 }
}​ 

 My first attempt put credentials next to db instead of inside it. CAP silently ignored the path and created db.sqlite instead. After cds deploy and cds watch , the log confirmed the right setup: connect to db > sqlite { url: 'db/practice.db' } 

 Production profile: SAP HANA Cloud 
 After checking that my HANA Cloud instance was running, I deployed with: mbt build
cf deploy mta_archives/practice_1_1.0.0.mtar 
 mbt build runs cds build --production , so the [production] block applies and the tables and CSV data go into an HDI container. 
 The production crash 
 The tables were created, but the app kept crashing. cf logs <app> --recent showed: Error: Cannot find '@sap/xssec' 
 In production, CAP switches authentication from dummy to jwt , and jwt needs @SAP /xssec . The log line just before it, connect to db > hana , showed the database side was fine. For this trial demo I set the auth kind explicitly, as in the config above, then rebuilt and redeployed. A real application should use XSUAA with @SAP /xssec instead. 
 I also pressed Ctrl+C mid-deploy, which left a failed operation open. cf mta-ops showed it, and cf deploy -i <id> -a abort cleared it. 
 Are the two databases independent? 
 Yes. I changed one book name in SQLite only. The local app showed the new name, while the deployed app, reading from HANA, kept the original. The CSV is only the starting point for each database. 

 Key takeaways 
 Profiles let one codebase use SQLite in development and HANA in production. 
 SQLite is in-memory unless you give it a path inside requires.db.credentials . 
 Production has different defaults, so test it early. 
 cds env requires.db --profile production shows which database a profile resolves to. Conclusion Using CAP profiles lets you maintain a single codebase while switching smoothly between a local SQLite file for development and SAP HANA Cloud for production. By configuring database credentials properly and explicitly managing authentication defaults like @sap/xssec , you can avoid common Cloud Foundry deployment errors. Once deployed, both environments run independently, giving you a safe local setup to test changes before pushing to the cloud. 
 Your turn 
 How do you handle authentication and database setup between development and production in your CAP projects? I’d love to hear your approach in the comments.
